# UI explanations in human language

When explaining UI bugs, review findings, or closed-set choices about screens:

1. Name what the human sees first: the square preview, the remove cross, the eye or download icon, the green badge, the filename bubble on hover — not internal props (`openerChild`, `endIcon`, `capture host`, `chip`).
2. If a technical name is needed, put it in backticks after the plain phrase once: the remove cross (`ThemedIconButton`).
3. Decision options must say what changes on screen and for whom (desktop hover versus phone tap), not only which component wraps which.
4. Prefer one short concrete example (“tap the PDF thumb on a phone”) before architecture.
5. Never open a clarify question with undefined jargon stacks joined by slashes.

Companion rules: `plain-language-chat` (full words and full sentences) and `backend-logic-explanations-human-language` (server-side behaviour in business terms). This rule only covers on-screen wording.
